  "notes": [
    "The deployer's initReserve (0x25d91ed8) for BUSD with fToken 0xdc9cdb40c8c105e9a12f422b2353777ee2bca2d2 and debt token 0xc3bc948a65f41518514a0894116cfb3b9f67ddde listed the reserve the anchor borrowed: getConfiguration(BUSD) turns nonzero at this block and is unchanged through block 9965526.",
    "Sum of MainPool Borrow(reserve, user, onBehalfOf, amount, ...) logs (topic0 0x7752d0a9...) with user != onBehalfOf, excluding the 45 WBNB borrows (2,519.06 WBNB, blocks 9895394-9959340) routed through the Neko deployer's helper 0xfd21385c8753a52157dbaebac805c1df1818d5da, where the transaction sender is the debtor. That leaves 196 attacker borrows (blocks 9965527-9971112), whose per-account counts match the Maze post-mortem: 1 + 103 via 0xe4ecb6c4 for hacker 1, 62 for 0x4f5bc386, 14 via 0x52c52cee, 12 for 0x3551fd33, 3 for 0x79391843 and 1 for 0x6666666c. Its per-asset totals equal the post-mortem's 'Total Loss (Debt Added)'. The borrowed underlying left the fTokens to the attackers; the victims were left with the debt. The attackers' later return of about 1.8M USD to the deployer is a recovery and is not deducted. BTCB is valued at the DefiLlama price nearest its borrow (0x28f2b77a, block 9970588); the stablecoins are valued at par.",
    "The Maze post-mortem notes that two attacker contracts predate MainPool's deployment and speculates that the attackers had code access; no insider attribution was published, and the loss is treated as an external exploit of the code defect.",
    "MainPool and its debt tokens were never verified; the missing delegation check is established from the anchor trace, the Borrow event fields and the post-mortem, not from source.",
    "The USDT, USDC and BTCB reserves that carry most of the loss were initialised at blocks 9873464, 9875084 and 9873775, all before the anchor; the knot follows the BUSD reserve the anchor exercised. Taking the latest of them, USDC at ts 1628494115, as the reset would shorten the age by 5,195 s."
  ]
